WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:42.020
[Music]

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:46.870
[A young girl rides her bike around a suburban cul-de-sac.]

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:52.350
[Narrator:] Sandra Stahl seems to be a normal healthy young girl.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:57.540
She can do everything her playmates do.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.780
[Sandra's mother calling out:] Sandra!

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:12.900
[Narrator:] But Sandra is a child apart. She has leukemia. Cancer of the blood.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.210
Sandra's leukemia has been brought under control by continuous doctor care.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.960
[Sandra and her mother walk into a hospital.]

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.370
At regular intervals she and her mother go to the hospital where a sample of her blood is taken.

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:29.770
The white cells in the sample of blood are counted to make sure that the disease is still under control.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:41.690
[Sandra's finger is pricked and blood taken.]

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:45.700
Some scientists believe that tiny particles called viruses,

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.190
possibly hidden deep inside the blood cells,

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:52.990
are involved in the onset of the disease. 

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:56.520
But can scientists prove that viruses are a cause of cancer?

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.980
[Machine sounds]

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:03.210
And with that knowledge, develop a cure?

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:06.430
[Image of microscopic cells.]

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.180
Thousands of scientists all over the world have been conducting research on the role of viruses in cancer.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.430
[Scientists are shown working in their labs.]

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:29.400
Two of those scientists have been asked if it is now possible to prove that viruses cause cancer. 

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:36.340
Dr. James Grace, Assistant Director of the Roswell Park Memorial Institute in Buffalo, New York,

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:43.120
and Dr. Sarah Stewart of the National Cancer Institute, discoverer of the polyomavirus.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:51.950
Before these two scientists answer the question, it's important to understand something about viruses and about cancer.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:53.490
[Close-up image of a cell.]

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:58.320
Cancer is a disease of the cells, the building blocks of which all living things are made.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.000
The rose, the robin, the rhinoceros.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:10.740
The microscope reveals different kinds of cells that make up the root of a plant,

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.420
or the muscle of a man,

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.790
or the skin of an animal,

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:22.040
but cells are more than building blocks; they're the very fabric of life. 

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:31.950
Each cell is a biological machine designed to ensure its own survival and the survival of the organism of which it is a part. 

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:38.880
To study living cells, researchers grow them in bottles.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:42.920
[Laboratory beakers and other equipment with cell material are shown.]

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.390
Each bottle is planted with healthy cells from normal tissue,

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:57.310
making the bottle in effect a substitute for the man or animal from which the cells were taken.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:03.740
To the bottle are added life-supporting chemicals and blood serum for food. 

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:08.510
These colonies of living cells are called tissue cultures.

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:17.400
Some colonies developed for research in the tissue culture section of the National Cancer Institute have survived in bottles for 25 years. 

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:25.280
The life processes of these cells can be recorded with time-lapse photography.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.370
[A scientist prepares a culture to be photograped, setting up equipment.]

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:38.770
A motion picture camera takes a series of still photographs through a microscope at long intervals of time. 

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:46.050
Events in a tissue culture bottle that normally take hours can be compressed into minutes. 

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:52.830
In time-lapse films, we see that cells, like higher animals, reach out for food, digest it, and eliminate waste. 

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:56.080
[Cell division activity is shown.]

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:03.630
And they reproduce; they divide, producing exact duplicates of themselves. 

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:08.190
A simple yet wondrous process, by which the essence of the cell can be sustained

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:15.800
for millions of generations, is a definition of life.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:21.450
But sometimes the life process is interrupted by agents of disease.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:28.220
For example, bacteria, which can be seen easily with the light microscope. 

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:34.460
It takes the electron microscope to reveal the smallest agents of disease known to man -- viruses.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.590
[A scientist peers into an electron microscope.]

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:42.560
Viruses are so small they can be measured in millionths of an inch.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:47.890
They are so elemental, they cannot reproduce without the help of a host cell,

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:49.930
yet they are potent agents of disease. 

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:51.830
[Successive close-ups of virus cells are shown and described.]

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:59.530
These viruses cause smallpox. Other viruses cause tobacco mosaic disease in plants.

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:05.820
These poliomyelitis viruses attack and destroy nerve cells in animals and humans.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:11.960
These viruses infect and destroy bacteria. 

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:18.770
Is there a virus that causes cancer? What is cancer? 

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:24.270
Time-lapse photography can show the effects of cancer on one living cell.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:30.330
A cancer cell, instead of producing normal daughter cells exactly like the parent cell, produces cells

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:39.080
that are sometimes too large, too many, and in other ways irregular. 

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:43.860
In other time-lapse films, by dividing the screen into two parts,

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:47.640
normal and cancerous growths can be compared.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:52.700
On the right, normal cells are reproducing normally and in sufficient numbers to maintain normal growth.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:56.070
[A split-screen shows normal versus cancerous cells.]

00:06:56.070 --> 00:07:02.970
On the left, the cancerous cells are reproducing wildly, out of control. 

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:12.980
The cancerous cells are overlapping and piling up on each other, producing abnormal tissue.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:17.540
The result of cancerous growth is shown in these two still photographs of human skin.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:19.650
[A split-screen shows damaged versus healthy tissue.]

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:26.420
In the photograph on the right, the cells near the surface of the normal skin are arranged in a normal functional pattern.

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:32.340
In the photograph on the left, the cancer cells have broken down the tissues and destroyed it. 

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:39.300
Several factors have been frequently associated with the onset of cancer, like polluted air.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:46.320
City dwellers who inhale polluted air, and cigarette smokers, suffer a very high incidence of lung cancer,

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:50.020
but why not all?

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:52.720
[Bomb sounds are heard and a mushroom cloud shown.]

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:56.760
X-rays and gamma radiation can produce cancer. 

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:01.120
When atomic bombs were dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki,

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:06.350
some survivors of the bombs were stricken with leukemia, but why not all? 

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:14.970
There must be another factor involved in leukemia and other cancers. Perhaps the virus. 

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:18.720
How do scientists prove that a virus causes disease?

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:21.550
In plants, as in all living things,

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:24.490
the traditional proof of the causation of disease follows three logical steps.

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:26.760
[A man is shown in a laboratory filled with plants.]

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:32.550
First, recovery of the suspected agent of disease from a sick host. 

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:34.600
This leaf has been infected by tobacco mosaic disease.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:37.200
[Close-up of infected leaf.]

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:45.070
The leaf, along with other infected leaves, is broken down in a laboratory solution,

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:52.560
filtered to remove large materials, then centrifuged at precisely the speed which will bring down the leaf cells, 

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:57.330
but leave the suspected agent of the disease suspended in the liquid above.

00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:01.380
Over a period of days the liquid goes through many similar purification steps,

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:03.630
[A centrifuge spins the plant material.]

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:09.060
until only the concentrated suspected agents remain. 

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:13.580
In the second step, the researcher inoculates a healthy leaf 

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:25.540
with the suspected agent of the disease by rubbing it into the surface.

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:30.560
If the new host gets the disease and the suspected agent is again recovered,

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:34.760
the three steps of the cycle have been completed and we have proved

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:42.800
that the disease is caused by the suspected agent, the tobacco mosaic virus. 

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.070
[White mice are shown scrambling over one another.]

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:53.000
In the same way, can we prove that viruses cause leukemia in mice?

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.720
Dr. Sarah Stewart will answer the question.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:58.010
[Dr. Stewart is shown in her lab.]

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:06.040
[Dr. Sarah Stewart:] Following the traditional three step recovery-reinfection cycle, we take a mouse with spontaneous leukemia: 

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:10.300
large glands, a big spleen, and big liver. 

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:22.650
Remove these cancerous organs, cut them into bits, and break them up in a standard solution.

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:30.940
We know heat will affect viruses, so we keep our sample cold. 

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:35.970
To remove heavy cellular material, we spin the sample in a refrigerated centrifuge.

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:37.920
[Dr. Stewart carries out the steps as she describes them.]

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:46.270
Spinning at 4,000 revolutions per minute for 20 minutes breaks down the solid material and leaves the viruses,

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:52.790
if there are any, in the apparently clear liquid. 

00:10:52.790 --> 00:11:02.750
We filter the liquid to make doubly sure that we have removed all cells.

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:19.600
With our cell-free tissue extract, we inoculate newborn mice.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:25.410
One of the reasons why we use mice for research is that they mature very rapidly

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:31.100
and can be inbred to produce special characteristics. 

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:40.580
After 90 days, some of the mice which we inoculated show symptoms of leukemia.

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:48.950
Large gland, big spleen and big liver, the same symptoms of the disease in the original mouse. 

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:52.970
Finally, we try to recover the suspected particle

00:11:52.970 --> 00:12:00.970
and sometimes we try to substantiate our proof by photographing the virus in a sample of the tissue. 

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:07.290
To prepare our sample for electron microscopy, technicians fix it in a chemical,

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:13.130
wash it, embed it in plastic, and bake the plastic for 24 hours. 

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:16.450
The specimen is then sliced to within a few millionths of an inch.

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:18.640
[Dr. Stewart continues to demonstrate each step as she describes it.]

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:32.250
The slices fall into a water bath, are teased apart by a brush with one or two bristles.

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:34.520
[Sound of heavy door sliding open.]

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:41.070
The electron microscope is buried deep in the sub-basement away from all vibrations.

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:50.510
A tiny copper grid holding the slice specimen is placed in a special airtight holder,

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:55.410
then slipped into an airlock. 

00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:59.940
The barrel of the electron microscope is at a near-vacuum.

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:04.460
Inside, a gun shoots a beam of electrons through the specimen,

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:07.670
painting a picture of it on a television tube

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:12.810
where it can be viewed or photographed by the electron microscopist. 

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:20.330
After thousands of fruitless searches, the electron microscopists have finally found and photographed

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:26.340
the very particle that we now know causes leukemia in mice.

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:35.360
The photographs which magnify the particles hundreds of thousands of times show that the particles bud from the cell wall. 

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:39.400
The center is made up of nucleic acid like the genetic material found in the cell.

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:42.300
[Cell images from the electron microscope are shown.]

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:47.330
The coat is similar to the wall of the cell from which this made. 

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:54.400
The virus pinches itself off and slips into the spaces between the cells

00:13:54.400 --> 00:14:02.480
where it joins other virus particles which are ready to infect other cells, perhaps causing disease. 

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:07.870
We know that two or three particles sometimes join like Siamese Twins,

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:10.120
but we don't know why,

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:15.050
and there are other mysteries of the life and evolution of these virus particles,

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:20.380
but we have proved that they can cause leukemia in mice. 

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:23.840
But leukemia is only one form of cancer.

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:26.930
Many cancers are solid tumors. 

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:34.050
Can we prove that viruses are involved in the causation of this parotid tumor?

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:38.160
In order to demonstrate viruses in this particular tumor,

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:50.210
we must increase the amount of the virus by packaging the tumor cells in tissue culture. 

00:14:50.210 --> 00:14:55.340
We inoculate these flasks with extract from the tumor of our sick brown mouse,

00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:57.910
[Dr. Stewart use a long syringe-like instrument to inject the extract.]

00:14:57.910 --> 00:15:07.620
exposing the healthy normal cells in the tissue culture to the cancerous cells.

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:11.520
We allow them to incubate for five to seven days...

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:22.130
again, at body temperature to simulate living conditions in a real animal. 

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:27.630
With the culture fluid, we can infect and cause disease in another 

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:32.330
healthy brown mouse which develops tumors of the salivary glands

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:35.430
like those of the original mouse. 

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:43.360
When we look at the sections of these organs which have been placed on carefully prepared slides under another optical microscope,

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:46.240
we see the typical evidence of cancer.

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:50.920
Sick cells with irregular shapes and big nuclei.

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:57.970
Biological proof; there is no longer any question that viruses cause cancer.

00:15:57.970 --> 00:16:06.310
Not only leukemia but solid tumors in four-legged animals.

00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:12.220
[Narrator:] But what about the higher animals? The two-legged kind that plays in the sun?

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:14.080
[Doctor:] How have you been feeling?

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:14.880
[Sandra:] Good.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:18.330
[Doctor:] Good. Do you get tired very easily?

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:20.810
[Sandra:] Yeah.

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:22.870
[Doctor:] Her appetite has been fine? No troubles?

00:16:22.870 --> 00:16:24.280
[Sandra's mother:] No.

00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:31.520
[Doctor:] Can I feel your tummy? I promise not to tickle.

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:34.440
[Narrator:] Dr. James Grace will answer the question: 

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:46.380
Can scientists prove that viruses cause human leukemia and other human cancers?

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:48.860
[Dr. James Grace:] At Roswell Park Memorial Institute,

00:16:48.860 --> 00:16:54.910
part of our research program is devoted to the growth of human cancer cells and tissue culture. 

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:57.530
This one here is the granddaddy of them all.

00:16:57.530 --> 00:17:03.100
This is the first human leukemic cell line to survive in long-term culture.

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:07.310
This was started in our laboratory in February of 1964.

00:17:07.310 --> 00:17:09.130
[Dr. Grace shows the vessel containing the cell line in a laboratory.]

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:12.650
Since then we have grown many other leukemic cell lines

00:17:12.650 --> 00:17:16.670
and we grow these in order to supply our own researchers,

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:24.350
as well as researchers in other laboratories around the world. 

00:17:24.350 --> 00:17:27.020
We like to think that we are helping patients like Sandra

00:17:27.020 --> 00:17:32.530
by treating her disease, but in a very real sense they are also helping us. 

00:17:32.530 --> 00:17:38.700
When they first come to the hospital, we take samples of their blood, which is full of leukemic cells.

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:43.830
These blood samples are then handled in sterile rooms to keep them as free as possible

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:49.090
from other diseases such as that caused by bacteria. 

00:17:49.090 --> 00:17:51.960
[The blood samples are handled and prepared for a centrifuge.]

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:56.150
The blood samples are then processed by centrifuging them.

00:17:56.150 --> 00:18:03.570
This brings down the heavier red cells and leaves the leukemic cells in the liquid above. 

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:10.960
One of the biggest problems has been to find the best chemical medium in which to grow the leukemic cells.

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:17.140
We tried literally hundreds of different combinations of medium over the past three years.

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:23.500
This one, the sixteen-hundred-and-fortieth one which we tried, finally worked. 

00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:31.340
It contains 52 ingredients all carefully weighed, measured, and filtered.

00:18:31.340 --> 00:18:37.160
The medium is then bottled for distribution to the various tissue-culture labs in the institute.

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:46.970
[The medium is handled and placed in vessels for distribution.]

00:18:46.970 --> 00:18:53.010
Several flasks of medium are inoculated with small amounts of blood from patients like Sandra.

00:18:53.010 --> 00:19:01.370
And a human leukemic cell line is then started, and hopefully it will grow long-term. 

00:19:01.380 --> 00:19:05.660
Quite a bit like a doctor making rounds of his own patients in a hospital,

00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:11.560
I go around frequently and look at our leukemic cell cultures and see how they are doing. 

00:19:11.560 --> 00:19:14.490
In the spinner lab, we try to increase the growth rate of the new cell line.

00:19:14.490 --> 00:19:16.660
[Doctors pull vessel from spinner and examine it.]

00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:21.620
The spinner keeps the cells rolling through the medium by exposing them to the chemicals

00:19:21.620 --> 00:19:29.090
and oxygen in the same way that the bloodstream might expose them to natural nutrients. 

00:19:29.090 --> 00:19:32.480
New cultures show only a few clumps of cells,

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:39.450
but when they are a few days old they show larger clumps.

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:43.240
To get even higher concentrations of white blood cells,

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:53.870
we use a semiautomatic device which we have termed a [tropa cell?]. 

00:19:53.870 --> 00:19:59.200
This solidly packed white culture that you see here, produced by the tropa cell,

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:02.630
is almost pure leukemic cells. 

00:20:02.630 --> 00:20:10.910
We can harvest as many as ten million leukemic cells per cubic centimeter of medium.

00:20:10.910 --> 00:20:13.210
We will soon have much larger tropa cells,

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:20.140
which instead of the one quart flask, we will use 50-gallon drums for growing the cells. 

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:24.170
Six of these large drums will be placed on this huge platform

00:20:24.170 --> 00:20:29.070
so that enormous quantities of human leukemic cells can be fed,

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:33.130
kept in chemical balance, and harvested automatically. 

00:20:33.130 --> 00:20:43.440
In the future, these vast quantities of cells might be used for vaccine. 

00:20:44.900 --> 00:20:51.320
At this time, the cells are kept in cold storage as much as 300 degrees below zero,

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:56.380
for use by laboratories around the world interested in leukemia research. 

00:20:56.380 --> 00:21:02.890
One of the samples of leukemic blood such as Sandra's might finally help to resolve the question: 

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:06.460
Do viruses cause cancer in human beings? 

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:12.180
We obviously cannot inject material from her blood into another child to find the answer,

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:17.810
but we can and, in fact, do, send material in special cold storage containers

00:21:17.810 --> 00:21:24.310
to a research laboratory where this material is injected into animals which are very similar to humans--the monkey. 

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:29.450
[A monkey is shown in an animal research lab.]

00:21:29.450 --> 00:21:36.130
One of the largest animal programs ever devoted to the search for the cause of a disease is now being conducted

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:39.900
with National Cancer Institute funds at the Biomedics Research Laboratory.

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:41.830
[Several incubators in a lab contain monkeys.]

00:21:41.830 --> 00:21:45.080
Monkeys come to this nursery within minutes after they are born,

00:21:45.080 --> 00:21:51.370
before they develop either immunity to disease or any diseases themselves. 

00:21:51.370 --> 00:21:57.760
Like the human infant, they are weighed after birth,

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:03.810
but unlike most human infants, they are rushed into a sterile environment.

00:22:03.810 --> 00:22:10.120
[Monkeys chatter as a baby monkey is enclosed in a plastic bag and dipped into a vat of solution.]

00:22:10.120 --> 00:22:12.540
They enter this environment through a special lock

00:22:12.540 --> 00:22:17.600
that washes and decontaminates everything that must be passed into the isolator. 

00:22:17.610 --> 00:22:23.820
As quickly as possible, they are inoculated with cancerous cells from patients like Sandra.

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:26.850
They rapidly become the healthiest monkeys in the world.

00:22:26.850 --> 00:22:31.040
Healthy because they live in a controlled environment with purified air

00:22:31.040 --> 00:22:33.310
at a constant temperature and humidity,

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:36.000
and they get plenty of tender loving care. 

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:39.450
The nurses treat them as if they were their own children.

00:22:39.450 --> 00:22:43.220
And they also take a formula that any pediatrician would approve.

00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:55.770
[Monkeys are fed with a bottle, held, and burped.]

00:22:55.770 --> 00:22:58.410
Any hint of illness brings the vet running. 

00:22:58.410 --> 00:23:06.010
Their temperature, pulse, blood pressure, and heartbeat are checked at regular intervals for any signs of abnormality.

00:23:06.010 --> 00:23:10.390
They are treated with kid gloves in the truest meaning of the word. 

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:16.850
For these monkeys may provide the answer to the question about human leukemia and viruses.

00:23:16.850 --> 00:23:23.050
From the sterile nursery, the young monkeys are then transferred to a sterile holding room. 

00:23:23.050 --> 00:23:29.300
Some of the monkeys inoculated two years ago are so far happy and apparently healthy,

00:23:29.300 --> 00:23:34.800
but if their disease follows a schedule like we would expect in human beings,

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:39.240
like we have observed with other experimental animals,

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:45.710
and if the materials with which they were inoculated, in fact, contained a cancer virus,

00:23:45.710 --> 00:23:50.840
then they should develop cancer at some point in their life. 

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:55.770
If they do, we will then have completed the infection cycle in primates.

00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:58.350
The next step in the traditional proof would then be 

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:03.680
recovery of the viruses from the monkeys with malignant disease.

00:24:03.680 --> 00:24:06.870
This, however, is only one of many approaches. 

00:24:06.870 --> 00:24:11.920
There are other bits of circumstantial evidence that viruses cause human leukemia.

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:18.010
This evidence is found in studies in such fields as immunology and electronmicroscopy.

00:24:18.010 --> 00:24:22.960
At Roswell Park Memorial Institute, the electron microscope has demonstrated 

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:25.920
the presence of suspected viruses in human leukemic cells.

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:37.420
[Close-up image of a cell, rotating.]

00:24:37.430 --> 00:24:42.720
[Narrator:] But what about the questions raised today by people like Sandra's mother, Mrs. Stahl?

00:24:42.720 --> 00:24:47.980
[Mrs.Stahl:] What progress has been made in taking care of it? 

00:24:47.980 --> 00:24:53.030
I know a cure they say is impossible right now.

00:24:53.030 --> 00:24:55.420
They hope it will come someday.

00:24:55.420 --> 00:25:00.980
[Doctor:] I think this has been one of the really bright spots in the management of malignant disease; 

00:25:00.980 --> 00:25:09.720
for example, about 1949 when the first leukemic drug, anti-leukemic drug, was developed,

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:17.500
the average time from diagnosis to death was about three months.

00:25:17.500 --> 00:25:21.560
In 1960, this has risen to about one year.

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:25.680
Now, at latest count just a few months ago,

00:25:25.680 --> 00:25:30.880
the average was in excess of 28 months and climbing quite rapidly.

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:36.570
[Mrs. Stahl:] I hear a lot of people wonder if it was contagious, or hereditary.

00:25:36.570 --> 00:25:40.910
[Doctor:] Based on our present knowledge, and this has been studied quite extensively,

00:25:40.910 --> 00:25:47.310
there is no evidence that leukemia has ever been contracted by contact.

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:53.120
In other words, there's no evidence of contagion like one has, for example, mumps, measles, chicken pox.

00:25:53.120 --> 00:25:55.830
[Mrs. Stahl:] What is being done about it now?

00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:59.340
[Doctor:] There's been a great deal of research into the cause of leukemia.

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:02.850
Now much of this has been based on animal studies,

00:26:02.850 --> 00:26:07.220
and one of the most exciting fields of leukemia research at present

00:26:07.220 --> 00:26:12.770
is the possible role of viruses in the causation of leukemia. 

00:26:12.770 --> 00:26:17.910
We know now that throughout the animal kingdom, many different animal species

00:26:17.910 --> 00:26:21.310
ranging all the way from the chicken upward,

00:26:21.310 --> 00:26:27.980
including cattle, dogs, mice, rats and so forth, that viruses can cause leukemia. 

00:26:27.980 --> 00:26:32.520
More importantly, one can get these viruses and grow them in a laboratory.

00:26:32.520 --> 00:26:34.710
These, for example, that cause mouse leukemia,

00:26:34.710 --> 00:26:40.760
it is possible to prevent the leukemia by means of an immunization procedure. 

00:26:40.760 --> 00:26:44.440
So this is very important from a practical standpoint

00:26:44.440 --> 00:26:49.910
in that if viruses can be shown to cause human leukemia,

00:26:49.910 --> 00:26:54.030
it is wholly within the realm of possibility that one could develop a vaccine

00:26:54.030 --> 00:26:58.420
which would both prevent and possibly assist in the treatment of it.

00:26:58.420 --> 00:27:00.850
So the animal models are there. 

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:05.010
We're now waiting for the real breakthrough in human studies.

00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:12.640
If, in fact, viruses do cause human leukemia and since the human is a member of the animal kingdom,

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:15.120
it would be very surprising to me personally,

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:20.040
 if he out of all the animals that we know were immune to viral causation of leukemia.

00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:24.040
[Sandra Stahl is shown pulling another child in a red wagon.]

00:27:24.040 --> 00:27:27.110
[Narrator:] In the long struggle for final proof,

00:27:27.110 --> 00:27:33.910
many scientists like Dr. Stewart and Dr. Grace believe that if viruses cause cancer in mice,

00:27:33.910 --> 00:27:37.740
they almost certainly cause it in monkeys and men.

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:43.620
The proof will be an important step toward the development of preventive vaccines

00:27:43.620 --> 00:27:48.860
and possibly the long-awaited cure for cancer.

00:27:51.840 --> 00:28:49.720
[Music]

